<h2>Outdoor Policy and Gear Info</h2>
<p>
	We are outside most of the day, in all kinds of weather, year-round, so having the right gear —
	and being able to put it on and take it off independently — is essential. The most important
	things to consider are keeping your child dry and giving them layering options. It's your
	responsibility to make sure your child has appropriate gear for the day; out of respect for
	prepared families, we're not able to stay inside because one family forgot their gear.
</p>
<p>
	Clothes should be comfortable, easy to move in, and easy for your child to put on and take off
	independently (please avoid snaps, buttons, and buckles for children over 18 months). We
	recommend keeping a backup pair of shoes at school, and boots plus several pairs of wool-blend
	socks for the wetter months. A medium/light layer like a hoodie or fleece should stay at school
	for cooler mornings, and rain gear should be clearly labeled waterproof — either a one-piece
	suit or a two-piece rain jacket and pants combo.
</p>

<h2>Tuition and Fees</h2>
<p>
	Enrollment for the school year begins with a $500 deposit that holds your child's slot. Tuition
	is due on the 1st of the month; payments not made in full by the 5th accrue a $25 late fee, and
	accounts unpaid by the 10th may result in your child being unable to return to care until the
	account is current. The first two weeks of care are a trial period during which either party may
	terminate without advance notice; after that, any changes to your contract, including
	termination, require 30 days' written notice.
</p>
<p>
	Children cannot be dropped off before 7:30am and must be picked up before 5:30pm; late
	pickups accrue a $1-per-minute fee. If you refer a family who enrolls, you'll receive a $50 credit
	during their second month of enrollment.
</p>

<h2>WPP Trading Days Policy</h2>
<p>
	We know families can't always attend on their scheduled days, and may sometimes need care on
	an unscheduled one. Through a day trader app built in partnership with one of our preschool
	families, you can freely buy, sell, or trade days directly with other families in our community.
	The transaction happens entirely between families — WPP simply needs to be notified once a day
	changes hands (either through the app or a message from the selling family) so we can update
	our attendance records accordingly. A child may only attend on a day they currently own.
</p>

<h2>Waitlist</h2>
<p>
	Families can be placed on our waitlist by submitting a completed application and a
	non-refundable $20 fee. Being waitlisted doesn't guarantee enrollment, but it does guarantee
	priority for open slots as they become available; we consider each family on the list with a goal
	of creating well-balanced cohorts and coordinating preferred schedules. Once offered a slot,
	families typically have up to a week to decide, and a $500 non-refundable deposit holds the slot
	for up to three weeks. Currently enrolled families have priority for schedule changes or enrolling
	a sibling, and aren't required to pay the $20 fee.
</p>

<h2>Health Policy</h2>
<p>
	Our health policy complies with the Oregon Health Department and Early Learning Department.
	We can care for mildly ill children, but there will be times your child can't attend, or needs to be
	picked up immediately after developing symptoms in care. In general, children need to stay
	home with a fever of 100.4°F or higher, diarrhea, vomiting, severe or persistent coughing or
	wheezing, an unmanageable runny nose, open sores discharging fluids, pink or crusty eyes, a new
	rash, extreme fatigue, or head lice. Return-to-school guidelines vary by symptom — generally 24
	to 48 hours symptom-free without medication, or a negative COVID test where applicable, or a
	doctor's note. We ask families to message us through our app with symptoms any time a child is
	kept home sick.
</p>
<p>
	We are no longer operating as Emergency Childcare as we were during the COVID pandemic, but
	we continue to maintain diligent handwashing, environmental sanitation, and extended time
	outdoors and with fresh air circulation, and we comply with current Oregon Health Authority and
	Child Care Division guidance on excluding children who are symptomatic or have been exposed.
</p>

<h2>Illness and Injury Policy</h2>
<p>
	It's likely that your child will pick up small cuts, scrapes, bumps, or bruises while in our care.
	Our general protocol is to wash cuts and scrapes with soap and water, apply Bactine and a
	bandage as needed, and use a cold washcloth or ice pack for bumps, bruises, or minor burns.
	Children developing a fever, vomiting, diarrhea, a new rash, or pink/gooey eyes while in care will
	be isolated from other children and you'll be asked to pick them up right away. We'll send minor
	incident reports through the app with a photo; any serious illness, injury, or incident will always
	be communicated with a phone call.
</p>

<h2>Immunization</h2>
<p>
	We require all students to be up to date on their required immunizations unless they have a
	medical waiver. Our program's vaccination rates are publicly available through Oregon's Child
	Care Vaccination Rates lookup tool by searching "Wild Pear Preschool."
</p>

<h2>Medication Policy</h2>
<p>
	We only administer prescription rescue medication (like inhalers, epi-pens, or Benadryl), brought
	in its original container with a completed Authorization for Medication Form. We do not
	administer non-rescue prescription or over-the-counter medication — parents are always welcome
	to come administer these on-site. We do ask families to sign an annual blanket permission slip
	for common topical items we apply frequently, like sunscreen (reapplied after nap on warm days),
	lotion, and Bactine for small cuts and scrapes. All medications are given to a teacher or director
	directly by a legal guardian and stored out of children's reach.
</p>

