<h2>Rest Time Policy</h2>
<p>
	After lunch, each child spends time resting on a cozy cot with a sheet and blanket. We begin
	with a read-aloud or quiet music, then give children time to rest independently and sleep if they
	need to; children who wake early may do quiet activities on their mat. As with toileting, we
	won't coerce a child to sleep or wake them early — our consistent message is that they don't
	have to sleep, but do need to rest quietly so their friends can. Families send a crib sheet and
	small blanket weekly, which we wash and return.
</p>

<h2>Meal Information</h2>
<p>
	Children are served breakfast, lunch, and an afternoon snack, included with tuition as part of
	our Food and Nutrition Curriculum, following USDA food program portion guidelines. Food is
	cooked onsite, prioritizing seasonal, local, and organic ingredients — including food grown
	onsite — and children participate in food prep, family-style serving, and clean-up. Children are
	always asked to try a small taste of each food offered, but ultimately decide what and how
	much they eat.
</p>
<p>
	We're happy to work with families on a plan for dietary restrictions due to allergies, medical
	conditions, or moral, cultural, or religious reasons. Modified meals confirmed necessary by a
	doctor for an allergy or medical condition are fully covered; other substitution requests are
	worked out collaboratively with the family.
</p>

<h2>Rules and Discipline Approach</h2>
<p>
	At Wild Pear we have four rules: Be Safe, Be Respectful, Be Kind, and Be Responsible. We believe
	there's no such thing as a "bad kid" or "bad behavior" — we view behavior as a child's attempt
	to get a need met, and partner with children to shift their expression toward what's safe and
	appropriate for school. We will never use corporal punishment, verbal assault, shaming, guilting,
	coercion, threats, or isolation to change a child's behavior, and don't use time-outs or bribes.
</p>
<p>
	Instead, we help children build a strong understanding of how to express needs and feelings and
	stay aware of others'. When conflicts arise between children, teachers stay close by to support
	restorative conversations, often centered on three simple questions: "Are you okay?", "What do
	you need to feel better?", and "Did that fix it?" If a child's behavior is regularly unsafe or
	requires excessive one-on-one attention, we'll work closely with the family to build a sustainable
	plan so the child can remain enrolled; if no workable plan can be found, we may need to
	unenroll the child.
</p>

<h2>Family Communication</h2>
<p>
	Our app is the best way to reach your child's teachers during the day — for planned absences,
	early pickups, questions about your child's day, or photo and incident updates. For anything
	more urgent, please text the directors on our shared family text line, which is provided directly
	to enrolled families; we'll use texting for unplanned closures and time-sensitive updates. For
	tuition matters, policy questions, or longer conversations, email or a scheduled meeting works
	best. We regularly send group emails with information you'll be responsible for knowing, so
	please make sure they aren't landing in your spam folder.
</p>
<p>
	We share photos of your child and their peers through the app and occasionally a shared Google
	Photos album, and let us know right away if you have any concerns about your child's photo
	being shared outside our currently enrolled community.
</p>

<h2>Family Involvement</h2>
<p>
	Your family is the number one source of education for your child, and we love partnering with
	you throughout the year. We host two family conferences (currently held over video call), occasional
	volunteer days to help keep our environment in great shape, and several fun, free family events
	including a Pumpkin Patch trip, a Parent's Night Out, Spring Fling, an end-of-year Campfire
	Night, and a beloved annual camping trip.
</p>

<h2>Birthdays at Wild Pear Preschool</h2>
<p>
	We celebrate each child's birthday with a special 15-20 minute birthday circle, walking the
	birthday child around a globe representing a trip around the sun for each year of their life while
	reflecting on photos and milestones from each age. Families are welcome, though not required,
	to attend — on the day of the circle, please send along a photo from each year of your child's
	life so far.
</p>

<h2>Plan for Meeting Potential Emergencies</h2>
<p>
	We stay alert to emergency warnings and follow the instructions of law enforcement and other
	designated authorities, using our training and best judgment to keep children safe until they can
	be reunited with parents. In any disaster or emergency, we first gather and account for all
	children in a safe space, then contact emergency services and text and call all custodial parents.
	If phones aren't working, we'll send emails and post updates on Facebook. The nearest hospital
	available to us is Providence Milwaukie Hospital (10150 SE 32nd Ave, Milwaukie, OR 97222,
	(503) 513-8300).
</p>
<p>
	<strong>Medical emergency:</strong> we contact emergency services, perform first aid/CPR, and ensure
	a teacher or parent stays with the child at all times, including during hospital transport, while
	other teachers keep the rest of the group safe and calm.
</p>
<p>
	<strong>Shelter in place:</strong> used for events like severe storms, power outages, or earthquakes.
	Teachers remain on-site with students, with access to attendance records, emergency paperwork,
	rescue medication, and an emergency supply kit, until children are picked up by a parent or
	authorized adult.
</p>
<p>
	<strong>Evacuation:</strong> used for events like flood, fire, or gas leak. Short-term evacuations move
	the group to a covered outdoor area on campus; longer evacuations move to a nearby site, with a
	note posted at the school, or to the Milwaukie City Planning Building (6101 SE Johnson Creek
	Blvd, Portland, OR 97206) or the Wichita Center (6031 SE King Rd, Milwaukie, OR 97222).
</p>
<p>
	<strong>Earthquake:</strong> we drop and take cover until shaking stops, then follow the evacuation
	procedure above. <strong>Lock down:</strong> used for an escalated threat where children may be in
	direct danger — children are moved into the building, lights turned off, and doors locked, while
	we follow the shelter-in-place procedure. <strong>Weather emergencies:</strong> we closely monitor
	extreme heat, cold, and air quality, bringing children inside or closing early as conditions
	require.
</p>

<h2>Certificates and Affiliations</h2>
<p>
	Our lead educators hold degrees in Early and Elementary Education, are Master Trainers with
	OCCD, and maintain current Infant and Child First Aid/CPR certification and food handler
	licensing. Wild Pear Preschool is certified by the Oregon Department of Early Education, holds a
	City of Milwaukie business license, is Clackamas County health inspected, and participates in the
	USDA Food Program. We're also affiliated with the Oregon Family Child Care Network, the
	Professional Resource Organization, and the Oregon Association for the Education of Young
	Children.
</p>

<h2>Mandated Reporting</h2>
<p>
	All teachers and directors are legally required to report concerns of child abuse and neglect,
	which includes children under age 5 being left alone in a car for any reason.
</p>

<h2>File a Complaint</h2>
<p>
	We are licensed as a Certified Family Child Care and abide by applicable Oregon State Rules and
	Regulations, available for review on request. If you have a question, complaint, or a problem, we
	would love to have a discussion with you directly. If you're not comfortable with that, or feel the
	matter is serious, you may make an official complaint to the Office of Child Care's Central Office
	at 1-800-556-6616, who will refer your information to a local child care licensing specialist.
</p>
